{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794561631474,"sessionID":"ses_e2bccb336ffeW5nAknN29DOzXD","part":{"partID":"prt_1d435633d001YprPWstrfzDEoB","sessionID":"ses_e2bccb336ffeW5nAknN29DOzXD","messageID":"msg_1d435475d0015OdtV9qTNt76Ca","type":"tool","id":"call_99a6b6a80a804de5a800c539","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md","limit":60},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md, lines 1-60\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Communication handoff\"\n3: description: \"End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"what do i do now\"\n6:   - \"end of turn handback\"\n7:   - \"next steps for the operator\"\n8:   - \"who does what next\"\n9:   - \"the reply ended with nothing to do\"\n10:   - \"buried the ask\"\n11:   - \"i asked in prose\"\n12:   - \"structured choice instead of prose\"\n13:   - \"offer options not paragraphs\"\n14:   - \"waiting on the user\"\n15:   - \"blocked on a decision\"\n16:   - \"needs your approval\"\n17:   - \"unanswered question at the end\"\n18:   - \"should i continue\"\n19:   - \"which option do you want\"\n20:   - \"handing control back\"\n21:   - \"the operator has to act\"\n22:   - \"nothing is blocked on you\"\n23:   - \"state restatement cadence\"\n24:   - \"where were we\"\n25:   - \"catch me up\"\n26:   - \"closing contract\"\n27:   - \"one concrete next action\"\n28:   - \"what is in flight\"\n29:   - \"still running in the background\"\n30:   - \"did it stall or is it working\"\n31:   - \"will you continue on your own\"\n32:   - \"waiting on me or on a machine\"\n33:   - \"resume condition\"\n34: importance_tier: important\n35: contextType: reference\n36: version: 1.6.0.8\n37: ---\n38: \n39: # Rule: Communication handoff\n40: \n41: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before ending a turn that leaves the operator anything to do.\n42: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n43: \n44: ## Fires when\n45: \n46: - About to end a turn, of any kind, substantive or not.\n47: - About to report that work is done, blocked, partially done, or waiting on something.\n48: - About to end a turn while something you started is still running.\n49: - About to ask the operator anything.\n50: - About to state a fork, a trade-off, or two acceptable paths.\n51: - About to continue past a decision that was never actually made, which is when this rule earns its load.\n52: \n53: ## The rule\n54: \n55: **End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it.**\n56: \n57: Naming is the obligation. An action the operator has to infer from a status report is an\n58: action that does not happen.\n59: \n60: ---\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 61]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md, lines 1-60\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Communication handoff\"\n3: description: \"End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"what do i do now\"\n6:   - \"end of turn handback\"\n7:   - \"next steps for the operator\"\n8:   - \"who does what next\"\n9:   - \"the reply ended with nothing to do\"\n10:   - \"buried the ask\"\n11:   - \"i asked in prose\"\n12:   - \"structured choice instead of prose\"\n13:   - \"offer options not paragraphs\"\n14:   - \"waiting on the user\"\n15:   - \"blocked on a decision\"\n16:   - \"needs your approval\"\n17:   - \"unanswered question at the end\"\n18:   - \"should i continue\"\n19:   - \"which option do you want\"\n20:   - \"handing control back\"\n21:   - \"the operator has to act\"\n22:   - \"nothing is blocked on you\"\n23:   - \"state restatement cadence\"\n24:   - \"where were we\"\n25:   - \"catch me up\"\n26:   - \"closing contract\"\n27:   - \"one concrete next action\"\n28:   - \"what is in flight\"\n29:   - \"still running in the background\"\n30:   - \"did it stall or is it working\"\n31:   - \"will you continue on your own\"\n32:   - \"waiting on me or on a machine\"\n33:   - \"resume condition\"\n34: importance_tier: important\n35: contextType: reference\n36: version: 1.6.0.8\n37: ---\n38: \n39: # Rule: Communication handoff\n40: \n41: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before ending a turn that leaves the operator anything to do.\n42: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n43: \n44: ## Fires when\n45: \n46: - About to end a turn, of any kind, substantive or not.\n47: - About to report that work is done, blocked, partially done, or waiting on something.\n48: - About to end a turn while something you started is still running.\n49: - About to ask the operator anything.\n50: - About to state a fork, a trade-off, or two acceptable paths.\n51: - About to continue past a decision that was never actually made, which is when this rule earns its load.\n52: \n53: ## The rule\n54: \n55: **End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it.**\n56: \n57: Naming is the obligation. An action the operator has to infer from a status report is an\n58: action that does not happen.\n59: \n60: ---\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 61]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md\",\"limit\":60}"},"time":{"start":1794561631037,"end":1794561631474}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794561631483,"sessionID":"ses_e2bccb336ffeW5nAknN29DOzXD","part":{"partID":"prt_1d43560f0002f3lhpCg1K2QPaz","sessionID":"ses_e2bccb336ffeW5nAknN29DOzXD","messageID":"msg_1d435475d0015OdtV9qTNt76Ca","type":"tool","id":"call_71b5898fbb104c339351513b","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md","limit":80},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md, lines 1-80\n1: ---\n2: title: Universal Patterns - Cross-Language Code Standards\n3: description: Patterns applicable to ALL languages in OpenCode system code including naming principles, commenting philosophy, and reference comment patterns.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"opencode universal patterns\"\n6:   - \"cross language naming principles\"\n7:   - \"commenting philosophy why\"\n8:   - \"reference comment patterns\"\n9: importance_tier: normal\n10: contextType: implementation\n11: version: 1.1.2.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # Universal Patterns - Cross-Language Code Standards\n15: \n16: Patterns applicable to ALL languages in OpenCode system code.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: This reference defines patterns that apply universally across JavaScript, TypeScript, Python, Shell, Rust, and JSON/JSONC in the OpenCode codebase. These principles ensure consistency regardless of language choice.\n25: \n26: ### When to Use\n27: \n28: - Before writing any new OpenCode system code\n29: - When reviewing code for consistency\n30: - When onboarding to OpenCode development patterns\n31: - As the foundation before applying language-specific rules\n32: \n33: ### Core Principle\n34: \n35: > **Clarity over cleverness.** Code is read far more often than written. Optimize for the reader, not the writer.\n36: \n37: ### Key Sources (Evidence)\n38: \n39: | Pattern | Source File | Line Reference |\n40: |---------|-------------|----------------|\n41: | Reference comments | `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/lib/search/folder-discovery.ts` | Near file top and import blocks |\n42: | Section organization | `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/core/config.ts` | Numbered divider blocks |\n43: | Naming conventions | `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/lib/config/spec-doc-paths.ts` | Throughout |\n44: | Python docstrings | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py` | Module and function docstrings |\n45: \n46: ---\n47: \n48: ## 2. NAMING PRINCIPLES\n49: \n50: ### Descriptive Names\n51: \n52: Names should describe what something IS or DOES without requiring context:\n53: \n54: **Good** (JS/TS use camelCase, Python/Shell use snake_case):\n55: ```javascript\n56: // JavaScript\n57: calculateMemoryScore()\n58: validateInputLengths()\n59: handleMemorySearch()\n60: ```\n61: ```typescript\n62: // TypeScript (same as JS for functions; PascalCase for types)\n63: calculateMemoryScore()\n64: validateInputLengths()\n65: interface SearchResult { }\n66: type MemoryState = 'active' | 'archived';\n67: ```\n68: ```python\n69: # Python\n70: calculate_memory_score()\n71: validate_input_lengths()\n72: handle_trigger_lookup()\n73: ```\n74: \n75: **Bad**:\n76: ```\n77: calcScore()\n78: validateLengths()\n79: handle()\n80: ```\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 81]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md, lines 1-80\n1: ---\n2: title: Universal Patterns - Cross-Language Code Standards\n3: description: Patterns applicable to ALL languages in OpenCode system code including naming principles, commenting philosophy, and reference comment patterns.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"opencode universal patterns\"\n6:   - \"cross language naming principles\"\n7:   - \"commenting philosophy why\"\n8:   - \"reference comment patterns\"\n9: importance_tier: normal\n10: contextType: implementation\n11: version: 1.1.2.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # Universal Patterns - Cross-Language Code Standards\n15: \n16: Patterns applicable to ALL languages in OpenCode system code.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: This reference defines patterns that apply universally across JavaScript, TypeScript, Python, Shell, Rust, and JSON/JSONC in the OpenCode codebase. These principles ensure consistency regardless of language choice.\n25: \n26: ### When to Use\n27: \n28: - Before writing any new OpenCode system code\n29: - When reviewing code for consistency\n30: - When onboarding to OpenCode development patterns\n31: - As the foundation before applying language-specific rules\n32: \n33: ### Core Principle\n34: \n35: > **Clarity over cleverness.** Code is read far more often than written. Optimize for the reader, not the writer.\n36: \n37: ### Key Sources (Evidence)\n38: \n39: | Pattern | Source File | Line Reference |\n40: |---------|-------------|----------------|\n41: | Reference comments | `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/lib/search/folder-discovery.ts` | Near file top and import blocks |\n42: | Section organization | `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/core/config.ts` | Numbered divider blocks |\n43: | Naming conventions | `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/lib/config/spec-doc-paths.ts` | Throughout |\n44: | Python docstrings | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py` | Module and function docstrings |\n45: \n46: ---\n47: \n48: ## 2. NAMING PRINCIPLES\n49: \n50: ### Descriptive Names\n51: \n52: Names should describe what something IS or DOES without requiring context:\n53: \n54: **Good** (JS/TS use camelCase, Python/Shell use snake_case):\n55: ```javascript\n56: // JavaScript\n57: calculateMemoryScore()\n58: validateInputLengths()\n59: handleMemorySearch()\n60: ```\n61: ```typescript\n62: // TypeScript (same as JS for functions; PascalCase for types)\n63: calculateMemoryScore()\n64: validateInputLengths()\n65: interface SearchResult { }\n66: type MemoryState = 'active' | 'archived';\n67: ```\n68: ```python\n69: # Python\n70: calculate_memory_score()\n71: validate_input_lengths()\n72: handle_trigger_lookup()\n73: ```\n74: \n75: **Bad**:\n76: ```\n77: calcScore()\n78: validateLengths()\n79: handle()\n80: ```\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 81]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md\",\"limit\":80}"},"time":{"start":1794561630448,"end":1794561631483}}}}
